---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { projects, tracks } from "../data/site";
import { cite } from "../lib/bibliography";
import { url } from "../lib/url";
const byId = (id: string) => projects.find((p) => p.id === id)!;
---

<Base>
	<section class="header" id="projects">
		<div class="container">
			<h1>Programming foundations for distributed software</h1>
			<p class="lead">
				Bismuth is a research project of the{" "}
				<a href="https://www.stg.tu-darmstadt.de/">Software Technology Group</a> at TU Darmstadt. It covers three directions:{" "}
				<span class="strand track-teal">distributed reactive programming</span>,{" "}
				<span class="strand track-magenta">replicated data types and protocols</span>, and{" "}
				<span class="strand track-orange">trust without a central authority</span>.
			</p>
			<div class="summary">
				<div class="card card-padded summary-card">
					<h3>Replication is automatic</h3>
					<p>Applications use ordinary data types and reactive signals. Replication and synchronization are handled by the platform, not by application code.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card card-padded summary-card">
					<h3>Local-first operation</h3>
					<p>Every device works on its local copy and stays usable offline. Coordination is added only where an application needs stronger guarantees.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card card-padded summary-card">
					<h3>Correct by composition</h3>
					<p>Replicated data types, reactive dataflow, and access control can be combined. Properties of the individual parts also hold for the combination.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<details class="card summary-details">
				<summary>Research overview</summary>
				<div class="abstract-body">
				<p>
					Distributed applications are built in one of two ways. Either the developers write the communication
					themselves, and then also have to solve consistency, fault tolerance, and security for each application. Or they
					use cloud services, which handle these problems but are non-programmable black-boxes that route all
					data through the provider.
				</p>
				<p>
					Bismuth explores a third option. Distribution is handled by programming abstractions that developers already
					already use. Replicated data types are ordinary algebraic data types, and the merge of concurrent changes is
					derived from their structure. Reactive signals describe how values depend on each other, and updates propagate
					along these dependencies across devices. Access control and encryption are part of the replicated data itself,
					so no trusted server is needed.
				</p>
				<p>
					Devices keep working on their local copy without a network connection. Changes are synchronized when possible,
					and all copies converge to the same state. Where an application needs stronger guarantees, say to keep an
					invariant such as a non-negative balance, coordination is added only for the affected operations. Consensus
					protocols can be built from the same replicated data types.
				</p>
				<p>
					The building blocks are meant to be combined. Properties such as convergence, or the safety of a protocol, are
					proven for each block, often machine-checked in Lean, so that programs built from them inherit these guarantees.
					The timeline below lists the projects and papers.
				</p>
				</div>
			</details>
			<h2 class="timeline-title">Research timeline</h2>
			<div class="timeline">
				{
					projects.map((p) => (
						<article class="project" id={p.id}>
							<div class="year">{p.year}</div>
							<h3>{p.title}</h3>
							<div class="tags">
								{p.tracks.map((t) => <span class:list={["tag", `track-${tracks[t].color}`]}>{tracks[t].title}</span>)}
							</div>
							<p>
								{p.summary}
								{p.links?.map((l) => <> <a href={l.href.startsWith("/") ? url(l.href) : l.href}>{l.label} ↗</a></>)}
							</p>
							{p.related && (
								<p class="related">
									Builds on:{" "}
									{p.related.map((id, i) => (
										<>
											{i > 0 && ", "}
											<a href={`#${id}`} title={byId(id).title}>{byId(id).short}</a>
										</>
									))}
								</p>
							)}
							{p.publications.length > 0 && (
								<ul class="pubs">
									{p.publications.map((pub) => {
										const e = cite(pub.key);
										return (
											<li>
												<span class="venue">{pub.venue}</span>: {e.url ? <a href={e.url}>{e.title}</a> : e.title}
												<div class="authors">
													{e.authors.join(", ")}
													{pub.artifact && <> · <a href={cite(pub.artifact).url}>research artifact</a></>}
												</div>
											</li>
										);
									})}
								</ul>
							)}
						</article>
					))
				}
			</div>
			<p class="all-pubs"><a href={url("/publications/")}>All publications →</a></p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section bg-indigo">
		<div class="container">
			<h2>Get involved</h2>
			<p class="lead">
				Bismuth is developed at the Software Technology Group of TU Darmstadt. To learn more about our research or the Bismuth library, you may join our Matrix channel.
			</p>
			<div class="buttons">
				<a class="hero" href="https://matrix.to/#/#difosys:matrix.tu-darmstadt.de">Join the chat</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
